('Dragón rojo, cría', 'Red Dragon, Wyrmling', 'Dragon, True', 'Dragón', 'Dragón mediano (fuego)', 4.0, '4', '7d12+14 (59 pg)', '+0', '40 pies, volar 150 pies (mala)', '16 (+6 natural), contacto 10, desprevenido 16', '+7/+10', '+10 (según tamaño; ver tabla de ataques de dragón)', '+10 (según tamaño; ver tabla de ataques de dragón)', '', 'arma de aliento 2d10 (15)', 'Inmunidad a fuego, vulnerabilidad a frío', 'Fort +7, Ref +5, Vol +5', 'Fue 17, Des 10, Con 15, Int 10, Sab 11, Car 10', '', '', 'Montañas cálidas', 'Cría, Muy joven, joven, Juvenil, y Adulto joven: solitario o nidada (2-5); Adulto, Adulto maduro, Viejo, Muy viejo, Anciano, Wyrm, o Gran wyrm: solitario, pareja, o familia (1-2 y 2-5 crías)', 'Triple del estándar', 'Siempre caótico maligno', '8-9 DG', '+4', 'The small scales of a wyrmling red dragon are a bright glossy scarlet, making the dragon easily spotted by predators and hunters, so it stays underground and does not venture outside until it is more able to take care of itself. Toward the end of young age, the scales turn a deeper red, and the glossy texture is replaced by a smooth, dull finish. As the dragon grows older, the scales become large, thick, and as strong as metal. The neck frill and wings are an ash blue or purple-gray toward the edges, becoming darker with age. The pupils of a red dragon fade as it ages; the oldest red dragons have eyes that resemble molten lava orbs.

